**Current support.** Template directives work today, and the `$config` source modifier is delivered. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their template and source directives modify the following eligible construct.

### The two locations

Place a template directive in the template head to modify that template:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]
]

Place a source directive immediately before the declaration it modifies. The source directive occupies its own line:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

Source directives must remain attached to their target. Comment-only lines may intervene, but a blank physical line breaks attachment. A directive at the start of a file still modifies the following construct.

### Directive operands

A directive may have one optional compile-time operand in the form $name, $name value or $name key = value. It cannot have a type annotation or a function-call-style argument list; `$name(...)` is not directive syntax. Template heads retain their own slot names and separators, so `$before value` and `$after value` pass one bare operand.

The compiler registers these template directives: `$before`, `$after`, `$fresh`, `$slot`, `$in`, `$note`, `$todo`, `$doc`, `$raw`, `$md`, `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code`. `$config` is the source-only modifier and takes no operand.

Module entry selection uses the fixed @entry.moth filename, not a directive. Project and builder settings stay as named constants inside config.moth. `$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` binding as a build input: the binding name is the input key, the declared type is the receiving contract and the initializer is the missing-input fallback. Only a marked binding may omit its initializer.

See @../directives (Directives), @../project-structure/#project-config (Project config) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
